select 'bbbb0004-0000-4000-8000-000000000028', v.pos, v.title, v.kind, v.body::jsonb, v.notes, v.mins
from (values
  (1, 'Diagnostic: where are you now?', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"The SI base units of force, from F = ma, are…","options":["kg m/s","kg m/s^2","kg m^2/s^2","kg/s^2"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Mass (kg) times acceleration (m/s^2) gives kg m/s^2, which we name the newton — see ''Dimensional analysis''."},{"prompt":"A ball is dropped from rest and falls for 2 s. Using g = 10 m/s^2, how far does it fall?","options":["10 m","20 m","40 m","5 m"],"answer":1,"explanation":"s = ut + (1/2)at^2 = 0 + 0.5*10*2^2 = 20 m — see ''Kinematics strategies''."},{"prompt":"A book rests on a level table. The normal force from the table on the book is best described as…","options":["the reaction to the book''s weight, acting on the Earth","equal in size to the weight because the book is in equilibrium","always equal to the weight in every situation","a force the book exerts on itself"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In vertical equilibrium the upward normal force balances the weight; they are equal here but are not a Newton''s-third-law pair — see ''Newton''s laws & free-body diagrams''."},{"prompt":"A 2 kg trolley moving at 3 m/s collides and sticks to a 1 kg trolley at rest. Their common speed afterward is…","options":["1 m/s","2 m/s","3 m/s","1.5 m/s"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Momentum is conserved: 2*3 = (2+1)v, so v = 6/3 = 2 m/s — see ''Energy & momentum methods''."},{"prompt":"For an object in uniform circular motion, the net force points…","options":["forward, along the velocity","backward, opposing motion","toward the center of the circle","away from the center"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Centripetal acceleration v^2/r is directed inward, so the net force is inward too — see ''Circular motion & gravitation''."},{"prompt":"For a mass on a spring in simple harmonic motion, the acceleration is…","options":["constant","proportional to displacement and directed toward equilibrium","proportional to displacement and directed away from equilibrium","zero at the extremes of the motion"],"answer":1,"explanation":"SHM is defined by a = -omega^2 x: a restoring acceleration proportional to displacement — see ''Oscillations (SHM)''."},{"prompt":"Three resistors of 2 ohm each are connected in series. Their combined resistance is…","options":["2/3 ohm","6 ohm","2 ohm","0.67 ohm"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Series resistances add: 2 + 2 + 2 = 6 ohm — see ''Electric circuits''."},{"prompt":"A charge moving parallel to a magnetic field line experiences a magnetic force of…","options":["zero","qvB at right angles to v","qvB along v","qE"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The magnetic force is qvB sin(theta); when v is parallel to B, theta = 0 and the force vanishes — see ''Fields''."}]}'::jsonb, null, 10),
  (2, 'Dimensional analysis and estimation', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Check any equation for unit consistency, recover a formula''s shape from its dimensions, and make a fast order-of-magnitude estimate. Warm-up: from the diagnostic, what combination of base units equals one newton?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Every physical quantity has a dimension built from mass M, length L, and time T (with more for charge and temperature). A valid equation must be dimensionally homogeneous: both sides carry the same units, and you can only add quantities that share units. This gives you two powerful tools. First, unit-checking: if the units of your answer are wrong, you made an algebra error, guaranteed. Second, dimensional construction: if you know which quantities a result depends on, you can often assemble the only combination with the right units, fixing the formula up to a dimensionless constant. Estimation is the sibling skill: replace unknowns with round order-of-magnitude values, keep only powers of ten, and get an answer good to within a factor of a few — enough to sanity-check a full calculation."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Check v^2 = u^2 + 2as. Left side: (m/s)^2 = m^2/s^2. Right side: u^2 is m^2/s^2, and 2as is (m/s^2)(m) = m^2/s^2. Both sides are m^2/s^2, so the equation is dimensionally consistent. Construct the pendulum period. Suppose the period T depends on length L (m), mass m (kg), and g (m/s^2). To get a time, we need M^0 L^0 T^1. Mass cannot help since nothing else carries kg, so it drops out. The only combination of L and g giving seconds is sqrt(L/g): units sqrt(m / (m/s^2)) = sqrt(s^2) = s. So T is proportional to sqrt(L/g), and indeed T = 2*pi*sqrt(L/g). Estimate the number of breaths a person takes in a day. About 15 breaths per minute, 60 minutes per hour, 24 hours: 15*60*24 is roughly 2*10^4 breaths — order ten thousand."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Are the units of kinetic energy (1/2)mv^2 the same as those of work Fs? 2) What are the SI units of pressure, force over area? 3) A quantity depends only on speed v (m/s) and radius r (m); what single combination has units of acceleration? 4) Estimate the number of seconds in a human lifetime of about 75 years. 5) Check whether ''distance = speed * time^2'' can possibly be right. Answers: 1) Yes: (kg)(m/s)^2 = kg m^2/s^2, and Fs = (kg m/s^2)(m) = kg m^2/s^2 — both are joules. 2) N/m^2 = kg/(m s^2), the pascal. 3) v^2/r, since (m/s)^2 / m = m/s^2. 4) 75*365*24*3600 is roughly 2*10^9 seconds. 5) No: the right side is (m/s)(s^2) = m*s, not metres, so it is dimensionally impossible."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: dropping units mid-calculation and losing the free error-check they provide. Mistake: expecting dimensional analysis to fix the dimensionless constant — it gives the shape (sqrt(L/g)), never the 2*pi. Exit: (a) What are the base units of the joule? (b) If the two sides of your equation have different units, what does that tell you? Answers: kg m^2/s^2; the equation is definitely wrong — no correct equation is dimensionally inconsistent."}]}'::jsonb, 'Units are algebra: carry them through every line and the final unit is a free check on your working. If it comes out wrong, the physics is wrong.', 14),
  (3, 'Kinematics: choosing the right strategy', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Attack any constant-acceleration problem by listing s, u, v, a, t, then selecting the single equation that connects your knowns to the unknown. Warm-up: an object dropped from rest for 2 s under g = 10 m/s^2 — which quantity are you told is zero?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"For motion with constant acceleration in a straight line, five quantities describe everything: displacement s, initial velocity u, final velocity v, acceleration a, and time t. Four equations link them, each one missing a different variable: v = u + at (no s), s = ut + (1/2)at^2 (no v), v^2 = u^2 + 2as (no t), and s = (1/2)(u + v)t (no a). The strategy is mechanical and reliable: write down the values you know, mark the unknown you want, then choose the equation that includes your knowns and the target while excluding the quantity you were not given. Fix a positive direction first, so signs of velocity, acceleration, and displacement stay consistent — a downward g is negative if up is positive."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"A ball dropped from rest, g = 10 m/s^2, falls for t = 2 s. Knowns: u = 0, a = 10 m/s^2, t = 2 s; want s. No v involved, so use s = ut + (1/2)at^2 = 0 + 0.5*10*4 = 20 m. A car brakes from u = 20 m/s to rest v = 0 over s = 40 m; find a. No time given, so use v^2 = u^2 + 2as: 0 = 400 + 2a*40, giving a = -400/80 = -5 m/s^2 (negative, i.e. deceleration). A stone thrown straight up at u = 15 m/s under g = 10 m/s^2; time to the top where v = 0. No s needed, so v = u + at: 0 = 15 - 10t, t = 1.5 s. Projectile: launched at 20 m/s at 30 degrees; horizontal speed is 20*cos30 = 17.3 m/s (constant), vertical speed is 20*sin30 = 10 m/s, so time up is 10/10 = 1 s and total flight is 2 s."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) A car accelerates from rest at 3 m/s^2 for 4 s; find its final speed. 2) Same car — how far did it travel? 3) An object falls from rest and hits the ground at 30 m/s under g = 10 m/s^2; from what height? 4) A train decelerates from 25 m/s to 5 m/s in 10 s; find a. 5) A projectile launched horizontally at 8 m/s from a 20 m cliff (g = 10 m/s^2) — how long until it lands? Answers: 1) v = u + at = 0 + 3*4 = 12 m/s. 2) s = (1/2)at^2 = 0.5*3*16 = 24 m. 3) v^2 = 2as gives 900 = 2*10*s, s = 45 m. 4) a = (5 - 25)/10 = -2 m/s^2. 5) Vertical: 20 = (1/2)*10*t^2, t^2 = 4, t = 2 s (horizontal speed is irrelevant to the fall time)."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: mixing sign conventions so that an upward launch and downward gravity end up with the same sign. Mistake: using a constant-acceleration equation across a phase where the acceleration changes (e.g. before and after brakes are applied). Mistake: coupling horizontal and vertical projectile motion — they share only the time. Exit: (a) Which SUVAT equation omits time? (b) In projectile motion, what single quantity links the horizontal and vertical parts? Answers: v^2 = u^2 + 2as; the time of flight t."}]}'::jsonb, 'List your knowns and the one unknown first, then pick the SUVAT equation that contains exactly those five letters minus the one you do not care about.', 14),
  (4, 'Newton''s laws and free-body diagrams', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Isolate a body, draw every force on it, resolve into components, and apply F_net = ma along each axis. Warm-up: for a book resting on a table, name the two vertical forces acting on the book."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Newton''s second law, F_net = ma, is a vector statement: it holds separately along each axis you choose. The whole method rests on the free-body diagram. Pick one object, then draw every external force acting on it — weight mg down, normal force N perpendicular to the surface, tension T along ropes, friction f opposing relative sliding, applied pushes. Choose axes (often along and perpendicular to the surface or motion), resolve each force into components, and write F_net = ma for each axis. If the body is in equilibrium, a = 0 and the forces balance. Newton''s third law pairs act on different bodies, so they never both appear on one free-body diagram — a common trap. On an incline of angle theta, weight splits into mg sin(theta) down the slope and mg cos(theta) into the slope."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"A 2 kg block pulled along a frictionless floor by a horizontal 6 N force. Free-body: weight 20 N down, normal 20 N up (they cancel), applied 6 N horizontal. Horizontal axis: F_net = ma gives 6 = 2a, so a = 3 m/s^2. A 5 kg block on a frictionless 30-degree incline (g = 10 m/s^2). Along the slope the only unbalanced force is mg sin30 = 5*10*0.5 = 25 N, so a = 25/5 = 5 m/s^2 down the slope; perpendicular, N = mg cos30 = 43.3 N. Atwood-style: masses 3 kg and 2 kg over a frictionless pulley. Take the heavier side as positive: net driving force is (3 - 2)g = 10 N, total mass moved is 5 kg, so a = 10/5 = 2 m/s^2 and the tension in the string is T = 2*(10 + 2) = 24 N (from the light mass: T - 20 = 2a). Friction case: a 4 kg block pushed by 30 N with a friction force of 10 N; net force 20 N, so a = 5 m/s^2."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) A 10 N force accelerates a mass at 2 m/s^2; find the mass. 2) A 3 kg block on a frictionless 30-degree incline (g = 10 m/s^2) — find its acceleration along the slope. 3) A 2 kg object hangs from a rope in equilibrium; find the tension. 4) A 6 kg block experiences a 24 N push and 6 N of friction; find its acceleration. 5) On an incline, does the normal force equal the full weight? Answers: 1) m = F/a = 10/2 = 5 kg. 2) a = g sin30 = 10*0.5 = 5 m/s^2. 3) T = mg = 2*10 = 20 N. 4) a = (24 - 6)/6 = 3 m/s^2. 5) No — it equals mg cos(theta), which is less than mg for any tilt."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: putting a Newton''s-third-law reaction force on the same diagram as its partner — pairs act on different bodies. Mistake: assuming the normal force always equals the weight; it only does so when the surface is horizontal and there is no vertical push. Mistake: forgetting to resolve weight into components on an incline. Exit: (a) What does F_net equal for a body in equilibrium? (b) On a slope of angle theta, what is the component of weight along the slope? Answers: zero; mg sin(theta)."}]}'::jsonb, 'One body, one diagram: draw only the forces acting ON the chosen object, never the forces it exerts on others. Then resolve along and perpendicular to the motion.', 15),
  (5, 'Checkpoint 1', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"Which check instantly reveals that ''distance = speed * time^2'' is wrong?","options":["plugging in numbers","dimensional analysis of the units","drawing a graph","trying it experimentally"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The right side has units m*s, not metres, so it is dimensionally impossible."},{"prompt":"A ball dropped from rest falls for 3 s under g = 10 m/s^2. How far?","options":["30 m","45 m","90 m","15 m"],"answer":1,"explanation":"s = (1/2)at^2 = 0.5*10*9 = 45 m."},{"prompt":"A car brakes from 20 m/s to rest over 40 m. Its acceleration is…","options":["-5 m/s^2","-10 m/s^2","-2.5 m/s^2","-20 m/s^2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"v^2 = u^2 + 2as gives 0 = 400 + 80a, so a = -5 m/s^2."},{"prompt":"A 2 kg block on a frictionless floor is pulled by 6 N horizontally. Its acceleration is…","options":["12 m/s^2","3 m/s^2","0.33 m/s^2","6 m/s^2"],"answer":1,"explanation":"a = F/m = 6/2 = 3 m/s^2."},{"prompt":"On a frictionless 30-degree incline (g = 10 m/s^2), a block accelerates down the slope at…","options":["10 m/s^2","5 m/s^2","8.7 m/s^2","2.5 m/s^2"],"answer":1,"explanation":"a = g sin30 = 10*0.5 = 5 m/s^2."},{"prompt":"The dimensionally correct form for a pendulum''s period, up to a constant, is…","options":["sqrt(g/L)","sqrt(L/g)","L/g","L*g"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Only sqrt(L/g) has units of seconds; the full result is T = 2*pi*sqrt(L/g)."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (6, 'Energy and momentum methods', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Solve problems by conservation of energy and conservation of momentum, and know which to reach for. Warm-up: a 2 kg trolley at 3 m/s sticks to a 1 kg trolley at rest — is kinetic energy conserved in that collision?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Conservation laws let you skip the details of the forces and connect a before-state to an after-state. Momentum p = mv is conserved for any isolated system, so total momentum before equals total momentum after every collision or explosion. Mechanical energy — kinetic (1/2)mv^2 plus potential mgh — is conserved when only conservative forces (gravity, ideal springs) act; friction converts some into heat. Collisions come in two flavours: elastic ones conserve kinetic energy too, while inelastic ones (especially ''sticking together'') lose kinetic energy to deformation and heat but still conserve momentum. The strategy: choose your system, write the conserved quantity before = after, and solve. Reaching for energy is smart when heights and speeds appear but time and force do not; reaching for momentum is smart at collisions."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"A 2 kg trolley at 3 m/s sticks to a 1 kg trolley at rest. Momentum: 2*3 + 1*0 = (2+1)v, so v = 6/3 = 2 m/s. Check energy: before, (1/2)(2)(3^2) = 9 J; after, (1/2)(3)(2^2) = 6 J — 3 J lost, confirming it is inelastic. Energy on a drop: a 1 kg ball falls from h = 5 m (g = 10 m/s^2). mgh = (1/2)mv^2, so v = sqrt(2gh) = sqrt(2*10*5) = 10 m/s. A pendulum released from a height 0.2 m above its lowest point reaches v = sqrt(2*10*0.2) = 2 m/s at the bottom — mass cancels. Recoil: a 60 kg skater throws a 2 kg ball at 10 m/s; by momentum conservation 0 = 60*V + 2*10, so V = -20/60 = -0.33 m/s (she recoils backward)."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) A 4 kg cart at 5 m/s hits and sticks to a 1 kg cart at rest; find their speed. 2) How much kinetic energy is lost in that collision? 3) A 2 kg ball falls from 10 m (g = 10 m/s^2); find its landing speed by energy. 4) A 0.5 kg ball moving at 4 m/s — what is its kinetic energy? 5) A 1000 kg cannon fires a 5 kg shell at 400 m/s; find the cannon''s recoil speed. Answers: 1) 4*5 = 5v, v = 4 m/s. 2) Before (1/2)(4)(25) = 50 J; after (1/2)(5)(16) = 40 J; lost 10 J. 3) v = sqrt(2*10*10) = sqrt(200) = 14.1 m/s. 4) (1/2)(0.5)(16) = 4 J. 5) 0 = 1000V + 5*400, V = -2000/1000 = -2 m/s."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: assuming kinetic energy is conserved in a sticking collision — it never is; only momentum is. Mistake: forgetting to include heat lost to friction when applying energy conservation. Mistake: using energy for a collision where you actually need momentum. Exit: (a) Which quantity is conserved in every collision? (b) In a perfectly inelastic collision, what happens to kinetic energy? Answers: momentum; some is converted to heat and deformation, so kinetic energy decreases."}]}'::jsonb, 'Before choosing forces or energy, ask what is conserved. Momentum is conserved in every collision; kinetic energy only in elastic ones; total energy always, if you track every form.', 15),
  (7, 'Circular motion and gravitation', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Apply F = mv^2/r to circular motion and combine it with Newton''s law of gravitation for orbits. Warm-up: a car rounds a bend at constant speed — is it accelerating?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"An object moving in a circle of radius r at speed v has a centripetal acceleration a = v^2/r = omega^2 r directed toward the centre, where omega = v/r is the angular speed. By Newton''s second law the net inward force must be F = mv^2/r. The key move is to identify which real force provides it: for a car it is friction, for a conical pendulum it is a component of tension, for a satellite it is gravity. Newton''s law of gravitation gives the attractive force between masses as F = G m1 m2 / r^2. For a satellite in a circular orbit, gravity supplies the centripetal force: G M m / r^2 = m v^2 / r, which rearranges to the orbital speed v = sqrt(GM/r) — notice the orbiting mass m cancels, so orbital speed depends only on the central mass and radius."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"A 0.5 kg ball on a 2 m string swings in a horizontal circle at 4 m/s. Required centripetal force: F = mv^2/r = 0.5*16/2 = 4 N, supplied by the string tension. A car of mass 1000 kg rounds a bend of radius 50 m at 10 m/s; the friction needed is F = 1000*100/50 = 2000 N. Angular speed: a wheel spinning at v = 6 m/s at radius r = 0.3 m has omega = v/r = 20 rad/s. Orbit: for a satellite where GM/r = 4*10^7 m^2/s^2, the orbital speed is v = sqrt(4*10^7) = 6.3*10^3 m/s. Vertical circle at the top: minimum speed is when gravity alone provides the centripetal force, mg = mv^2/r, so v = sqrt(gr) — for r = 2.5 m and g = 10, v = sqrt(25) = 5 m/s."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) A 2 kg mass moves in a circle of radius 4 m at 6 m/s; find the centripetal force. 2) Find its centripetal acceleration. 3) A record spins at omega = 5 rad/s; what is the speed of a point 0.2 m from the centre? 4) If gravity provides a satellite''s centripetal force, what real force is the centripetal force? 5) A 1200 kg car takes a 60 m bend at 12 m/s; find the required friction. Answers: 1) F = 2*36/4 = 18 N. 2) a = v^2/r = 36/4 = 9 m/s^2. 3) v = omega*r = 5*0.2 = 1 m/s. 4) gravitational attraction between the satellite and the planet. 5) F = 1200*144/60 = 2880 N."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: adding a separate ''centripetal force'' to the free-body diagram on top of the real forces — the centripetal force IS the resultant of the real forces. Mistake: thinking constant speed means no acceleration; the direction changes, so there is centripetal acceleration. Mistake: keeping the orbiting mass in v = sqrt(GM/r) — it cancels. Exit: (a) In what direction does centripetal acceleration point? (b) For a satellite, which force provides the centripetal force? Answers: toward the centre of the circle; gravity."}]}'::jsonb, 'Centripetal force is not a new force — it is the NET inward force, supplied by whatever is physically present: tension, gravity, friction, or the normal force.', 15),
  (8, 'Oscillations: simple harmonic motion', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Recognise simple harmonic motion from its defining equation, and compute period, frequency, and energy for springs and pendulums. Warm-up: for a mass on a spring, at which point is the acceleration greatest?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A system is in simple harmonic motion when its acceleration is proportional to displacement from equilibrium and directed back toward it: a = -omega^2 x. Here omega is the angular frequency in rad/s, linked to period and frequency by omega = 2*pi/T = 2*pi*f. The motion is sinusoidal: x = A cos(omega t) with amplitude A. Two standard systems: a mass m on a spring of stiffness k gives omega = sqrt(k/m), so T = 2*pi*sqrt(m/k); a simple pendulum of length L for small swings gives omega = sqrt(g/L), so T = 2*pi*sqrt(L/g). Energy sloshes between kinetic and potential: it is all potential at the extremes (where speed is zero and acceleration is largest) and all kinetic at the centre (where speed is greatest v_max = omega*A and acceleration is zero). The period does not depend on amplitude — that is what makes oscillators good clocks."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"A 0.5 kg mass on a spring with k = 200 N/m. omega = sqrt(k/m) = sqrt(200/0.5) = sqrt(400) = 20 rad/s, so T = 2*pi/20 = 0.31 s. A pendulum of length L = 1 m (g = 10 m/s^2): T = 2*pi*sqrt(1/10) = 2*pi*0.316 = 1.99 s, about 2 s. Maximum speed: if that spring-mass has amplitude A = 0.1 m, v_max = omega*A = 20*0.1 = 2 m/s at the centre. Maximum acceleration: a_max = omega^2*A = 400*0.1 = 40 m/s^2 at the extreme. To halve a pendulum''s period you must quarter its length, since T is proportional to sqrt(L)."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) A mass on a spring has omega = 10 rad/s; find its period. 2) A 2 kg mass on a spring of k = 50 N/m — find omega. 3) A pendulum has length 0.4 m (g = 10 m/s^2); find its period. 4) A SHM oscillator has A = 0.05 m and omega = 8 rad/s; find v_max. 5) Where in the cycle is the acceleration zero? Answers: 1) T = 2*pi/10 = 0.63 s. 2) omega = sqrt(50/2) = sqrt(25) = 5 rad/s. 3) T = 2*pi*sqrt(0.04) = 2*pi*0.2 = 1.26 s. 4) v_max = omega*A = 8*0.05 = 0.4 m/s. 5) at the equilibrium position, where x = 0."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: dropping the minus sign in a = -omega^2 x, which loses the restoring nature that defines SHM. Mistake: thinking a bigger amplitude gives a longer period — period is independent of amplitude. Mistake: confusing the spring formula T = 2*pi*sqrt(m/k) with the pendulum''s T = 2*pi*sqrt(L/g). Exit: (a) What is the defining equation of SHM? (b) Where in the swing is the speed greatest? Answers: a = -omega^2 x; at the equilibrium point (the centre)."}]}'::jsonb, 'SHM is defined by one equation: a = -omega^2 x. The minus sign is the restoring force pointing back toward equilibrium — never drop it.', 14),
  (9, 'Checkpoint 2', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"A 3 kg cart at 4 m/s sticks to a 1 kg cart at rest. Their common speed is…","options":["2 m/s","3 m/s","4 m/s","1 m/s"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Momentum: 3*4 = (3+1)v, so v = 12/4 = 3 m/s."},{"prompt":"A 1 kg ball falls from 5 m (g = 10 m/s^2). By energy, its landing speed is…","options":["5 m/s","10 m/s","50 m/s","7.1 m/s"],"answer":1,"explanation":"v = sqrt(2gh) = sqrt(2*10*5) = sqrt(100) = 10 m/s."},{"prompt":"A 2 kg mass moves in a circle of radius 4 m at 6 m/s. The centripetal force is…","options":["9 N","18 N","3 N","72 N"],"answer":1,"explanation":"F = mv^2/r = 2*36/4 = 18 N."},{"prompt":"In uniform circular motion at constant speed, the object is…","options":["not accelerating","accelerating toward the centre","accelerating along its velocity","in equilibrium"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The velocity direction changes, giving a centripetal acceleration v^2/r toward the centre."},{"prompt":"A 0.5 kg mass on a spring with k = 200 N/m has angular frequency…","options":["20 rad/s","400 rad/s","10 rad/s","100 rad/s"],"answer":0,"explanation":"omega = sqrt(k/m) = sqrt(200/0.5) = sqrt(400) = 20 rad/s."},{"prompt":"Which change is guaranteed NOT to affect a pendulum''s period?","options":["increasing the length","moving to a planet with different g","increasing the amplitude for small swings","changing sqrt(L/g)"],"answer":2,"explanation":"For small swings the period is independent of amplitude; it depends only on L and g."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (10, 'Waves: speed, frequency, and superposition', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Use v = f*lambda, handle standing waves on strings and in pipes, and predict interference from path difference. Warm-up: if a wave''s speed stays the same but its frequency doubles, what happens to its wavelength?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"A wave carries energy without transporting matter. Its speed, frequency, and wavelength obey v = f*lambda, where f is set by the source and v is fixed by the medium. When a wave passes into a new medium, f is unchanged, so lambda scales with v. Superposition says overlapping waves add displacement by displacement. Two coherent sources interfere: where the path difference is a whole number of wavelengths (n*lambda) they arrive in phase and reinforce (constructive); where it is a half-integer number ((n + 1/2)*lambda) they cancel (destructive). Standing waves form when a wave reflects and overlaps itself, giving fixed nodes and antinodes. A string fixed at both ends of length L has a fundamental wavelength lambda = 2L, so f = v/(2L); a pipe open at both ends behaves similarly, while a pipe closed at one end has a fundamental lambda = 4L."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"A wave of frequency 50 Hz and wavelength 4 m travels at v = f*lambda = 50*4 = 200 m/s. Sound at 340 m/s with f = 170 Hz has lambda = v/f = 340/170 = 2 m. A guitar string of length 0.5 m carries waves at 300 m/s; its fundamental has lambda = 2L = 1 m, so f = 300/1 = 300 Hz. Interference: two speakers emit 1 m waves; at a point 3 m from one and 4 m from the other, the path difference is 1 m = 1*lambda, a whole wavelength, so the waves reinforce — you hear a loud spot. A pipe closed at one end, length 0.85 m, fundamental lambda = 4L = 3.4 m, so with sound at 340 m/s, f = 340/3.4 = 100 Hz."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) A wave has f = 20 Hz and lambda = 5 m; find its speed. 2) A 256 Hz sound travels at 340 m/s; find its wavelength. 3) A string of length 1 m carries waves at 400 m/s; find the fundamental frequency. 4) Two sources emit 2 m waves; a path difference of 3 m gives constructive or destructive interference? 5) If v doubles and f is unchanged, what happens to lambda? Answers: 1) v = 20*5 = 100 m/s. 2) lambda = 340/256 = 1.33 m. 3) lambda = 2L = 2 m, f = 400/2 = 200 Hz. 4) 3 m = 1.5*lambda, a half-integer number of wavelengths, so destructive (cancellation). 5) lambda = v/f doubles."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: thinking frequency changes when a wave enters a new medium — it is the wavelength that changes, frequency is fixed by the source. Mistake: using lambda = 2L for a pipe closed at one end, where the fundamental is actually 4L. Mistake: confusing constructive (whole-wavelength path difference) with destructive (half-wavelength). Exit: (a) State the wave equation linking v, f, and lambda. (b) A path difference of half a wavelength gives what kind of interference? Answers: v = f*lambda; destructive (cancellation)."}]}'::jsonb, 'Wave speed is set by the medium; frequency is set by the source. When a wave changes medium its frequency stays fixed and its wavelength adjusts so v = f*lambda still holds.', 14),
  (11, 'Electric circuits: a systematic approach', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Reduce resistor networks, apply Ohm''s law and Kirchhoff''s rules, and compute power. Warm-up: three 2-ohm resistors in series — what is their combined resistance?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Current I is the flow of charge; potential difference V is the energy delivered per unit charge; resistance links them by Ohm''s law V = IR. To analyse a network, first simplify: resistors in series carry the same current and add, R_series = R1 + R2 + ...; resistors in parallel share the same voltage and combine reciprocally, 1/R_parallel = 1/R1 + 1/R2 + .... Kirchhoff''s two rules handle anything: the junction (current) rule says currents into a node equal currents out (charge conservation); the loop (voltage) rule says the sum of voltage changes around any closed loop is zero (energy conservation). Power dissipated in a resistor is P = VI = I^2 R = V^2/R. The strategy: collapse series and parallel groups to find the total current from the source, then work back outward to find individual currents and voltages."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Three 2-ohm resistors in series across a 12 V battery: R = 6 ohm, so I = V/R = 12/6 = 2 A, and each resistor drops IR = 2*2 = 4 V. Two 6-ohm resistors in parallel: 1/R = 1/6 + 1/6 = 2/6, so R = 3 ohm. Mixed: a 4-ohm resistor in series with two 4-ohm resistors that are in parallel with each other. The parallel pair is 2 ohm, in series with 4 ohm gives 6 ohm total; across 12 V that is I = 2 A through the single resistor, which then splits equally, 1 A each, through the pair. Power: a 3-ohm resistor carrying 2 A dissipates P = I^2 R = 4*3 = 12 W. A device rated 60 W at 12 V draws I = P/V = 60/12 = 5 A."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) Two 3-ohm resistors in series across 12 V — find the current. 2) Two 4-ohm resistors in parallel — find the combined resistance. 3) A 10-ohm resistor carries 0.5 A; find the voltage across it. 4) A resistor dissipates 20 W while carrying 2 A; find its resistance. 5) A 100 W bulb runs at 200 V; find its current. Answers: 1) R = 6 ohm, I = 12/6 = 2 A. 2) 1/R = 1/4 + 1/4 = 1/2, R = 2 ohm. 3) V = IR = 0.5*10 = 5 V. 4) P = I^2 R gives 20 = 4R, R = 5 ohm. 5) I = P/V = 100/200 = 0.5 A."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: adding parallel resistances directly instead of adding reciprocals — parallel resistance is always less than the smallest branch. Mistake: assuming the same current flows through parallel branches; they share voltage, not current. Mistake: forgetting that the combined parallel resistance is smaller, not larger, than either resistor. Exit: (a) How do resistances combine in series? (b) What quantity is the same across two resistors in parallel? Answers: they add; the potential difference (voltage)."}]}'::jsonb, 'Two rules run every circuit: charge is conserved at junctions (currents in = currents out) and energy is conserved around loops (voltage rises = voltage drops). Everything else follows from these plus V = IR.', 15),
  (12, 'Fields: electric and magnetic', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Compute forces from electric and magnetic fields, and handle a charge moving through each. Warm-up: a charge moves parallel to a magnetic field line — how big is the magnetic force on it?"},{"heading":"The idea","text":"An electric field E is the force per unit charge, so a charge q in a field feels F = qE, along the field for a positive charge. A point charge Q sets up a field E = kQ/r^2 at distance r (with k = 8.99*10^9 N m^2 / C^2), and Coulomb''s law gives the force between two charges as F = k q1 q2 / r^2. A uniform field between parallel plates a distance d apart at voltage V is E = V/d. Magnetic fields act only on moving charge: a charge q moving at speed v at angle theta to a field B feels F = qvB sin(theta), maximum when motion is perpendicular (theta = 90 degrees) and zero when parallel (theta = 0). The force is perpendicular to both v and B, so it does no work — it changes direction, not speed, curving the charge into a circle. A current-carrying wire of length L in a field feels F = BIL."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"A charge q = 2 C in a field E = 5 N/C feels F = qE = 2*5 = 10 N. Parallel plates at V = 100 V separated by d = 0.02 m give E = V/d = 100/0.02 = 5000 N/C. Coulomb: two 1 microcoulomb charges (1*10^-6 C) at r = 0.1 m repel with F = 8.99*10^9 * (10^-6)^2 / (0.1)^2 = 8.99*10^9 * 10^-12 / 10^-2 = 0.899 N. Magnetic force: a charge q = 0.5 C moving at v = 4 m/s perpendicular to B = 2 T feels F = qvB = 0.5*4*2 = 4 N; if it moved parallel to B, the force would be 0. A wire carrying I = 3 A, length L = 0.5 m, in B = 0.4 T (perpendicular) feels F = BIL = 0.4*3*0.5 = 0.6 N."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) A 3 C charge sits in a field of 4 N/C; find the force on it. 2) Parallel plates at 200 V separated by 0.05 m — find the field strength. 3) A 0.2 C charge moves at 10 m/s perpendicular to a 0.5 T field; find the magnetic force. 4) The same charge now moves parallel to the field; find the force. 5) A wire of length 2 m carrying 5 A sits perpendicular to a 0.3 T field; find the force. Answers: 1) F = qE = 3*4 = 12 N. 2) E = V/d = 200/0.05 = 4000 N/C. 3) F = qvB = 0.2*10*0.5 = 1 N. 4) 0 N, since sin(0) = 0. 5) F = BIL = 0.3*5*2 = 3 N."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: applying F = qvB to a stationary charge — magnetic forces act only on moving charge. Mistake: forgetting the sin(theta) factor, so a charge moving along the field is wrongly given a force. Mistake: thinking the magnetic force speeds a charge up; being perpendicular to velocity, it does no work and only curves the path. Exit: (a) What is the force on a charge q in an electric field E? (b) When is the magnetic force on a moving charge zero? Answers: F = qE; when the velocity is parallel (or antiparallel) to the field, so sin(theta) = 0."}]}'::jsonb, 'A field is force per unit test object: electric field E is force per unit charge, so a charge in a field feels F = qE. Keep track of directions — fields are vectors.', 15),
  (13, 'Checkpoint 3', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"A wave has frequency 50 Hz and wavelength 4 m. Its speed is…","options":["12.5 m/s","200 m/s","54 m/s","0.08 m/s"],"answer":1,"explanation":"v = f*lambda = 50*4 = 200 m/s."},{"prompt":"A string fixed at both ends, length 0.5 m, carries waves at 300 m/s. Its fundamental frequency is…","options":["150 Hz","300 Hz","600 Hz","75 Hz"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Fundamental lambda = 2L = 1 m, so f = v/lambda = 300/1 = 300 Hz."},{"prompt":"Two 6-ohm resistors in parallel have combined resistance…","options":["12 ohm","3 ohm","6 ohm","0.33 ohm"],"answer":1,"explanation":"1/R = 1/6 + 1/6 = 2/6, so R = 3 ohm — less than either branch."},{"prompt":"A 3-ohm resistor carries 2 A. The power it dissipates is…","options":["6 W","12 W","1.5 W","18 W"],"answer":1,"explanation":"P = I^2 R = 4*3 = 12 W."},{"prompt":"A 0.5 C charge moves at 4 m/s perpendicular to a 2 T field. The magnetic force is…","options":["0 N","4 N","2 N","8 N"],"answer":1,"explanation":"F = qvB sin90 = 0.5*4*2 = 4 N."},{"prompt":"Parallel plates at 100 V separated by 0.02 m give a field of…","options":["2 N/C","5000 N/C","50 N/C","0.0002 N/C"],"answer":1,"explanation":"E = V/d = 100/0.02 = 5000 N/C."}]}'::jsonb, null, 8),
  (14, 'Capstone: a multi-step problem set', 'reading', '{"sections":[{"heading":"What you''ll be able to do","text":"Chain several principles across one problem — kinematics into energy, energy into circular motion, forces into fields — while tracking units at each hand-off. Warm-up: name the four questions you ask to open any problem."},{"heading":"The idea","text":"Hard problems are rarely hard physics; they are several easy steps stacked, where the answer to one stage feeds the next. The reliable opening routine is four questions. One, what am I given and what do I want? Two, which principle connects them — a conservation law, a force balance, a field relation? Three, does the algebra give the right units? Four, is the answer sensible in the limits (does it grow the right way, and are the magnitudes plausible)? Work symbolically as far as you can, substitute numbers last, and keep the unit trail. The worked examples below each combine two or three of the course''s principles; treat them as templates for how to decompose, not results to memorise."},{"heading":"Worked examples","text":"Loop-the-loop. A ball is released from rest at height h on a frictionless track and enters a vertical loop of radius r = 2 m; find the minimum h to keep contact at the top (g = 10 m/s^2). Stage 1, circular motion: at the top the minimum condition is gravity alone providing the centripetal force, mg = mv^2/r, so v^2 = gr = 20 m^2/s^2. Stage 2, energy: the drop from h to the top of the loop (height 2r) supplies the kinetic energy, mg(h - 2r) = (1/2)mv^2, so g(h - 2r) = (1/2)v^2. Substitute: 10(h - 4) = 0.5*20 = 10, so h - 4 = 1, h = 5 m. Units check: every term is an energy per unit mass (m^2/s^2). Projectile plus energy. A stone leaves a 20 m cliff horizontally at 15 m/s (g = 10 m/s^2); find its speed on landing. Stage 1, kinematics for the fall time: 20 = (1/2)*10*t^2, t = 2 s; vertical landing speed v_y = g*t = 20 m/s. Stage 2, combine components: horizontal 15 m/s is unchanged, so speed = sqrt(15^2 + 20^2) = sqrt(225 + 400) = sqrt(625) = 25 m/s. Charge in a field then curving. A charge q = 1 C, mass 0.01 kg, is accelerated from rest through V = 200 V (gaining energy qV), then enters a magnetic field. Stage 1, energy: qV = (1/2)mv^2, so 200 = 0.5*0.01*v^2, v^2 = 40000, v = 200 m/s. Stage 2, the field of B = 0.5 T perpendicular then exerts F = qvB = 1*200*0.5 = 100 N, curving it — the force sets the radius via 100 = mv^2/R, R = 0.01*40000/100 = 4 m."},{"heading":"Your turn","text":"1) A 2 kg ball falls from 5 m onto a spring (g = 10 m/s^2); find its speed just before impact. 2) A stone is thrown horizontally at 10 m/s from a 45 m cliff; find the fall time (g = 10 m/s^2). 3) For that stone, find its vertical speed at landing. 4) For that stone, find its total landing speed. 5) A 0.5 kg mass swings on a 1 m string; released from the horizontal, find its speed at the bottom by energy (g = 10 m/s^2). Answers: 1) v = sqrt(2gh) = sqrt(100) = 10 m/s. 2) 45 = (1/2)*10*t^2, t^2 = 9, t = 3 s. 3) v_y = g*t = 10*3 = 30 m/s. 4) sqrt(10^2 + 30^2) = sqrt(1000) = 31.6 m/s. 5) mgh = (1/2)mv^2 with h = 1 m gives v = sqrt(2*10*1) = sqrt(20) = 4.47 m/s."},{"heading":"Common mistakes & exit ticket","text":"Mistake: plugging numbers in too early, so an algebra slip cannot be caught by a symbolic unit-check. Mistake: dropping a stage — for the loop, forgetting the top of the loop sits at height 2r above the bottom. Mistake: adding component speeds directly instead of by Pythagoras. Exit: (a) What are the four opening questions? (b) For a ball to keep contact at the top of a vertical loop, what supplies the centripetal force at the minimum speed? Answers: what do I know / want, which principle connects them, do the units work, is the limit sensible; gravity alone (mg = mv^2/r)."}]}'::jsonb, 'Multi-step problems reward the same habit throughout: name the principle for each stage, pass the output of one stage as the input to the next, and unit-check at every hand-off.', 16),
  (15, 'Comprehensive final quiz', 'quiz', '{"questions":[{"prompt":"The SI base units of the joule are…","options":["kg m/s^2","kg m^2/s^2","kg m^2/s^3","kg m/s"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Energy = force * distance = (kg m/s^2)(m) = kg m^2/s^2."},{"prompt":"A car brakes from 30 m/s to rest over 90 m. Its deceleration magnitude is…","options":["5 m/s^2","3 m/s^2","10 m/s^2","15 m/s^2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"v^2 = u^2 + 2as: 0 = 900 + 2a*90, a = -5 m/s^2."},{"prompt":"On a frictionless incline, the acceleration along the slope is g times…","options":["cos(theta)","sin(theta)","tan(theta)","1"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The weight component along the slope is mg sin(theta), so a = g sin(theta)."},{"prompt":"A 5 kg cart at 4 m/s sticks to a 3 kg cart at rest. Their common speed is…","options":["2.5 m/s","4 m/s","1.5 m/s","3.2 m/s"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Momentum: 5*4 = (5+3)v, v = 20/8 = 2.5 m/s."},{"prompt":"A satellite''s circular orbital speed v = sqrt(GM/r) does NOT depend on…","options":["the central mass M","the orbital radius r","the satellite''s own mass m","G"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Setting GMm/r^2 = mv^2/r cancels m, so orbital speed is independent of the satellite''s mass."},{"prompt":"A 1 kg mass on a spring of k = 100 N/m has period…","options":["0.63 s","10 s","0.1 s","6.3 s"],"answer":0,"explanation":"omega = sqrt(100/1) = 10 rad/s, so T = 2*pi/10 = 0.63 s."},{"prompt":"A sound wave at 340 m/s has frequency 170 Hz. Its wavelength is…","options":["0.5 m","2 m","510 m","1.7 m"],"answer":1,"explanation":"lambda = v/f = 340/170 = 2 m."},{"prompt":"Two 4-ohm resistors in parallel, in series with a 2-ohm resistor, give a total of…","options":["10 ohm","4 ohm","6 ohm","2 ohm"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The parallel pair is 2 ohm; 2 + 2 = 4 ohm total."},{"prompt":"A charge moving parallel to a magnetic field experiences a force of…","options":["qvB","zero","qE","BIL"],"answer":1,"explanation":"F = qvB sin(theta) and sin(0) = 0, so the force is zero."},{"prompt":"Which single check can prove an equation wrong without any numbers?","options":["substituting large values","dimensional (unit) consistency","graphing it","asking a peer"],"answer":1,"explanation":"If the two sides carry different units, the equation cannot be correct."},{"prompt":"A ball needs minimum speed at the top of a vertical loop of radius r. That speed is…","options":["sqrt(2gr)","sqrt(gr)","gr","sqrt(g/r)"],"answer":1,"explanation":"At the minimum, gravity alone is centripetal: mg = mv^2/r, so v = sqrt(gr)."},{"prompt":"A stone thrown horizontally at 15 m/s off a cliff lands after a 2 s fall (g = 10 m/s^2). Its landing speed is…","options":["25 m/s","20 m/s","35 m/s","15 m/s"],"answer":0,"explanation":"v_y = 20 m/s, horizontal 15 m/s, so speed = sqrt(15^2 + 20^2) = sqrt(625) = 25 m/s."}]}'::jsonb, null, 12)
) as v(pos, title, kind, body, notes, mins)
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.lessons l
  where l.course_id = 'bbbb0004-0000-4000-8000-000000000028' and l.position = v.pos
);
